> > John, that was my first thought as well for subtracting something from t; but here t is the index for year and they've subtracted t/2 from t (so if you have 50 years and you take t - t/2 for year 1, you get 0.5, not -24 as you would want if you were centering). To center the year value, you'd have to do t - n/2 instead, if n is your total number of years. Perhaps the book authors will enlighten us as to what we're missing. =)
